WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:10.400
In April 2003, the Human Genome&nbsp;
Project was declared completed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:14.880
putting an end to the 13-year long&nbsp;
scientific endeavor. What is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:22.000
human genome project? What is inside the human&nbsp;
genome? And, how to sequence the human genome?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.080
These are some of the questions that&nbsp;
we will try to address in this lecture.

00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:34.400
The Human Genome Project is an international&nbsp;
research effort to sequence and map all of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:41.120
genes of Homo sapiens. The project began in&nbsp;
1990 initially headed by James Watson at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:47.360
U.S. National Institutes of Health. Largely due to&nbsp;
his disagreement with his boss, Bernadine Healy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.680
over the issue of patenting genes,&nbsp;
he was forced to resign in 1992.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.160
He was replaced by Francis Collins in April&nbsp;
1993, and the name of the Center was changed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:04.480
the National Human Genome Research Institute&nbsp;
in 1997. A working draft of the genome was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:10.080
released in 2000 and a complete one in 2003,&nbsp;
with further analysis still being published.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:16.160
In May 2006, another milestone was passed&nbsp;
on the way to completion of the project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:19.680
when the sequence of the last chromosome&nbsp;
was published in the journal Nature.
 &nbsp;

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:26.800
Completed in April 2003, the Human Genome Project&nbsp;
gave us a complete representative sequence of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.800 --> 00:01:32.320
human genome. The representative sequence is&nbsp;
a composite from several people who donated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:39.440
blood samples. Originally, close to 100 people&nbsp;
volunteered to give a sample of their blood. Each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:44.320
person provided their informed consent, affirming&nbsp;
that they agreed to the study of their DNA.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:50.160
No names were attached to the blood samples and&nbsp;
ultimately scientists used only a few of them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.640
These measures ensured that the&nbsp;
DNA sequences remained anonymous.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:59.200
Not even the donors knew whether their&nbsp;
samples were actually used or not.
 &nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:09.360
Although the human genome sequencing was&nbsp;
declared completed by the end of 2003, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:15.840
are a number of regions of the human genome that&nbsp;
are unfinished. Small gaps that are unrecoverable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.800
in any current sequencing method remain, amounting&nbsp;
for about 1 percent of the gene-containing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:27.040
portion of the genome. These include:
 
• The central regions of each chromosome, known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:32.240
centromeres, which consists of highly repetitive&nbsp;
DNA sequences that are difficult to sequence.

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:42.480
• The ends of the chromosomes, called&nbsp;
telomeres, which are also highly repetitive.

00:02:42.480 --> 00:02:46.560
• There are also several loci&nbsp;
in each individual’s genome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:49.840
that contain repetitive sequence&nbsp;
that are difficult to sequence.

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:19.680
The strategy originally established by&nbsp;
the publicly funded Human Genome Project&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:25.120
was based on a method called hierarchical&nbsp;
shotgun sequencing method. In this approach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:31.760
genomic DNA is cut into pieces of about 150&nbsp;
megabases and inserted into a bacterial artificial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.400
chromosomes vector, and transformed into E.&nbsp;
coli, where they are replicated and stored.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:41.840
The bacterial artificial chromosomes inserts&nbsp;
were isolated and mapped to determine the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:48.640
order of each cloned 150 megabases fragment.&nbsp;
This is referred to as the Golden Tiling Path.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:55.200
Each bacterial artificial chromosomes fragment&nbsp;
in the Golden Path is fragmented randomly into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.200 --> 00:04:01.280
smaller pieces, typically 1 to 2 kilobases long,&nbsp;
and each piece is cloned into a plasmid vector.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:07.280
The resulting transformed bacterial colonies are&nbsp;
picked at random and sequenced on both strands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.240 --> 00:04:13.920
These sequences are aligned so that identical&nbsp;
sequences are overlapping. These contiguous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:19.040
pieces are then assembled into finished sequence&nbsp;
once each strand has been sequenced about 4 times.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:28.080
The term contig refers to a known DNA&nbsp;
sequence that is contiguous and lacks gaps.

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:33.600
In 1998, a parallel project was conducted&nbsp;
by the private company Celera Genomics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:38.720
Celera used a riskier technique called&nbsp;
whole genome shotgun sequencing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.720 --> 00:04:42.480
which had been used to sequence&nbsp;
bacterial genomes that smaller in size&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.560
and contain less repetitive DNA. Shotgun&nbsp;
sequencing randomly shears genomic DNA into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:53.840
small pieces which are cloned into plasmids and&nbsp;
sequenced on both strands, thus eliminating the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:58.960
bacterial artificial chromosomes step from the&nbsp;
publicly funded Human genome Project approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:04.320
Once the sequences are obtained, they are&nbsp;
aligned and assembled into finished sequence.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:12.000
The advantage to the hierarchical approach is&nbsp;
sequencers are less likely to make mistakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.160
when assembling the shotgun fragments&nbsp;
into contigs as long as full chromosomes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:22.560
The reason is that the chromosomal location for&nbsp;
each bacterial artificial chromosomes is known,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:28.480
and there are fewer random pieces to assemble. The&nbsp;
disadvantage to this method is time and expense.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.440 --> 00:05:34.640
The whole genome shotgun method used by&nbsp;
Celera Genomics is faster and less expensive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:38.560
but it is more prone to errors due to&nbsp;
incorrect assembly of finished sequence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:42.240
It also required tremendous computational power. 
 &nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:48.080
Which method is better? It depends on&nbsp;
the size and complexity of the genome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.880 --> 00:05:53.120
With the human genome, each group believes&nbsp;
its approach to be superior to the other.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:59.520
It is worth mentioning that Celera had access to&nbsp;
the publicly funded Human Genome Consortium data&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:03.280
but the Human Genome Consortium did&nbsp;
not have access to the Celera data.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:09.680
Indeed, without the Golden Tiling Path released&nbsp;
by the Human Genome Consortium, the whole genome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:14.480
shotgun method will not be able to determine where&nbsp;
the fragment of the sequenced DNA belongs to.
 &nbsp;

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:18.720
Because of the advantages and&nbsp;
disadvantages of both approaches,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.720 --> 00:06:23.040
an idealized strategy evolved into&nbsp;
hybrids towards the end of the project,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:28.160
in which the Human Genome Consortium selected&nbsp;
more clones arbitrarily and Celera made use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.160 --> 00:06:32.960
of the bacterial artificial chromosomes&nbsp;
maps and sequence generated by the HGP.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:39.680
In that way, both organizations were able to reach&nbsp;
their goals in less than the expected time frame.

00:06:42.800 --> 00:06:49.920
The total estimated size of the human genome is&nbsp;
3.2 gigabases. Most DNA in the human genome is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.920 --> 00:06:55.920
non-coding DNA, including intergenic regions,&nbsp;
introns, repetitive sequences and so forth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.960 --> 00:07:05.280
About 28% of human DNA is transcribed into RNA and&nbsp;
only a mere 1.25% is actually codes for proteins.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:11.600
On average, the introns are longer in human&nbsp;
DNA than in other organisms sequenced so far.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.640 --> 00:07:19.280
Over half the human genome consists of&nbsp;
repeated sequences. Some 45% is transposons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.240 --> 00:07:26.000
3% of the human genome consists of repeats&nbsp;
of just a few bases such as microsatellites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:32.720
Variable Number Tandem Repeats and etc. And 5%&nbsp;
of the human genome is made up of duplications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:38.960
of large genome segments. There are both AT-rich&nbsp;
regions and GC-rich regions in the human genome.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:43.680
Curiously, the zones of GC-rich&nbsp;
sequence have a higher density of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:48.480
genes and the introns are shorter. The&nbsp;
significance of this is still unknown.

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:56.480
One of the biggest surprise in the human genome&nbsp;
is the revelation that humans only have around&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.480 --> 00:08:03.120
25,000 genes rather than the previously&nbsp;
estimated 100,000 genes. The nematode worm,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:09.040
Caenorhabditis, with approximately 18,000 genes,&nbsp;
has half as much genetic information as humans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.920 --> 00:08:13.280
The mouse, have essentially the&nbsp;
same number of genes as humans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:18.720
Thus, the genome size is not directly&nbsp;
related to its biological complexity.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:24.400
Another surprise of the human genome is that&nbsp;
only a third of the genome code for protein.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:29.120
The other two thirds are DNA that we are&nbsp;
yet to find out what their function is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:34.480
These findings have significant implications&nbsp;
on how we understand the basic science of how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.480 --> 00:08:39.840
life works, but also how those sequences, which&nbsp;
are regulatory, can affect health and disease.

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:47.520
However, biological complexity may&nbsp;
presence in the expressed protein:
 &nbsp;

00:08:48.320 --> 00:08:54.560
First, alternative splicing of a pre-mRNA can&nbsp;
yield multiple functional mRNAs corresponding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.560 --> 00:09:01.200
to a particular gene. In human, about 60% of the&nbsp;
transcript are subject to alternative splicing. 
 &nbsp;

00:09:02.240 --> 00:09:07.760
Second, variations in the post-translational&nbsp;
modification of some proteins may produce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.760 --> 00:09:13.840
functional differences. For example, 80&nbsp;
to 90% of human proteins are acetylated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:21.120
Reversible phosphorylation of proteins is another&nbsp;
important regulatory mechanism. Reversible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:26.320
phosphorylation results in a conformational change&nbsp;
in the structure in many enzymes and receptors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.320 --> 00:09:32.640
causing them to become activated or deactivated. 
 
Finally, qualitative differences in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.640 --> 00:09:37.040
interactions between proteins and their&nbsp;
integration into pathways may contribute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:43.200
significantly to the differences in biological&nbsp;
complexity among organisms. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:48.080
signals from the exterior of a cell are mediated&nbsp;
to the inside of that cell by protein-protein&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.080 --> 00:09:53.920
interactions of the signaling molecules. This&nbsp;
process, called signal transduction, plays a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.920 --> 00:10:00.480
fundamental role in many biological processes and&nbsp;
in many diseases. Proteins might interact for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.480 --> 00:10:06.240
long time to form part of a protein complex, a&nbsp;
protein may be carrying another protein, or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:12.560
protein may interact briefly with another protein&nbsp;
just to modify it. Therefore, protein-protein&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:17.840
interactions are of central importance for&nbsp;
virtually every process in a living cell.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:23.040
Although the Human Genome&nbsp;
Project is considered completed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:25.520
there are great challenges&nbsp;
to understand what it means.

00:10:26.560 --> 00:10:30.640
There is still 1% of the genome at the&nbsp;
heterochromatin region unsequenced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:32.720
which may or may not harbor any gene.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.680 --> 00:10:37.680
This challenge will only be overcome by the&nbsp;
development of new sequencing technique.

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:41.920
Also, the regulatory signals for most genes remain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.920 --> 00:10:47.920
uncharacterized. It is currently unclear how&nbsp;
epigenetic modifications such as cytosine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.920 --> 00:10:53.520
methylation and gene silencing on a genome-wide&nbsp;
scale, determine their biological consequences.

00:10:54.480 --> 00:11:00.720
The human genome sequence also identified more&nbsp;
than 1.4 million single nucleotide polymorphisms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.680 --> 00:11:07.280
Although any two unrelated people share&nbsp;
about 99.9% of their DNA sequence,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:12.000
some people may have an A at a particular site&nbsp;
on a chromosome while others have a G instead.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.040 --> 00:11:18.240
Further work is currently being conducted to find&nbsp;
genetic variants affecting health, disease and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:24.160
response to drugs and environmental factors.
 
The scientific challenges outlined above focus&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:29.520
on how the genome sequence can be mined&nbsp;
for biological information. Ultimately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:29.520 --> 00:11:34.320
it is the function of the gene product that&nbsp;
plays the integral role in the biological system.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.200 --> 00:11:41.840
Therefore, tremendous effort has also been&nbsp;
carried out to decipher the gene function.

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:50.560
In summary, the availability of a complete genome&nbsp;
sequence will enormously facilitate the solution&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.560 --> 00:11:55.200
of the more difficult problem of identifying&nbsp;
the genetic components of the more complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.200 --> 00:12:02.640
and more common disorders, such as many forms&nbsp;
of diabetes, asthma, cancer, and mental illness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.360 --> 00:12:09.440
in which multiple genetic and environmental&nbsp;
factors interact. Using techniques that can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.440 --> 00:12:14.320
measure the expression of thousands of genes&nbsp;
at a time, scientists are now beginning to look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:17.600
globally for differences in gene expression&nbsp;
that are associated with, for example, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:23.840
ability to respond to different drugs&nbsp;
or pathological states such as cancer

